# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for known false positives.
#
# Companion to cmake/valgrind.supp. Three places point
# `TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=` at this file, and they are the only places this
# repository sets TSAN_OPTIONS at all:
#
#   * the `clang-tsan` **ctest preset** in CMakePresets.json, through
#     `${sourceDir}` so it resolves from any working directory -- a relative
#     path does not survive test discovery, which that preset's description
#     explains at length (morph#688);
#   * the `Test` step of ci.yml's `linux-sanitizers` job;
#   * the `Test` step of ci.yml's `kanban-tsan` job.
#
# All three carry the same value, and that value is
# `suppressions=<this file>:second_deadlock_stack=1` -- colon-joined, because a
# second `TSAN_OPTIONS` key replaces the first and would silently drop this
# file. Anything added here has to be added in all three, or a local
# reproduction stops matching the CI run it is reproducing.
#
# ── What this file is not, and what second_deadlock_stack=1 buys ───────────
#
# Both points below are about morph#578, an intermittent `lock-order-inversion`
# seen once in six full `morph_tests` runs whose stack was lost, and they are
# written here because this is the file the next person will open.
#
#   1. Every `race:` entry below is a **suppression**, so none of them could
#      have hidden that report: TSan matches `mutex:`/`deadlock:` entries
#      against lock-order findings, and there are none here. The issue says
#      this and the issue is right. Whether TSAN_OPTIONS was set at the time
#      is irrelevant to whether the warning could appear.
#
#   2. `second_deadlock_stack=1` **is** set (morph#736 set it, in all three
#      places above), and what it buys is measured rather than assumed. A
#      23-line standalone control -- two static mutexes taken in both orders,
#      clang 22.1.8, x86-64 Linux -- reports the inversion either way and
#      prints *both* `acquired here while holding` stacks either way, so the
#      option is **not** why morph#578's report was uninformative; a
#      `grep -A 60 | head -70` over the run is, as that issue states. What it
#      adds, exactly, is 24 further lines carrying two more stacks (47 report
#      lines -> 71 on that control; 36 -> 55 on the two-mutex inversion
#      measured for the preset):
#
#          Mutex M0 previously acquired by the same thread here:
#          Mutex M1 previously acquired by the same thread here:
#
#      i.e. where the *already-held* mutex of each pair was taken. morph#578's
#      cycle has three mutexes, two of them at stack addresses, and those two
#      stacks are what would name the fixture or short-lived object holding
#      them. Without the option TSan prints only `Hint: use
#      TSAN_OPTIONS=second_deadlock_stack=1 to get more informative warning
#      message` -- advice nobody can take after the fact, because morph#578 and
#      morph#717 are intermittent and the run that fires is the only evidence
#      that will ever exist. It costs nothing measurable: 2M lock acquisitions
#      under TSan took a median 0.275s without it and 0.273s with (morph#736).
#
# So: do not drop it while tidying a preset or a workflow env block. It is not
# decoration, and the report that needs it cannot ask for it in advance.
#
# ── libstdc++'s refcounted exception teardown (morph#476) ──────────────────
#
# A `std::exception_ptr` is a refcounted handle to one heap-allocated
# exception object, and the `std::runtime_error` inside it holds its message
# in libstdc++'s refcounted COW `std::string`.
# `SocketBackend::onDisconnected()` builds a single `DisconnectedError` and
# `cancelPending()` hands that one `exception_ptr` to every drained pending
# completion, so N `CompletionState`s share it; each destructor rethrows it,
# reads `what()` for the orphan log, and then drops its reference, on
# whatever thread happened to drop the last `Completion` handle.
#
# The last reference to go frees the exception object and its message
# buffer. TSan reports the `what()` read on one thread and that free on
# another as a data race. It is not one: the ordering edges are those two
# refcounts, decremented with `__exchange_and_add_dispatch(..., -1)` under
# `__ATOMIC_ACQ_REL`. A thread reading `what()` provably still holds a
# reference, so the free provably cannot precede the read. TSan cannot see
# either edge because both live inside an **uninstrumented**
# `libstdc++.so.6`: the tool sees an opaque library decrement something and
# then call `free()`, with no happens-before to order the two accesses.
#
# Established with a 33-line standalone control -- several threads sharing
# one `exception_ptr`, each rethrowing it and reading `what()` while the
# parent drops its reference -- in which a use-after-free is impossible by
# refcounting and which reproduces the identical `operator delete` and
# `free` summaries 20 runs out of 20, with no morph code linked at all. Its
# write stacks carry both shapes seen in the wild: one through
# `exception_ptr::_M_release`, one through the COW string's
# `_M_dispose`/`_M_destroy` under `~runtime_error`.
#
# The behaviour is on `origin/master` too; this branch's new `SocketBackend`
# disconnect test is simply the first thing in the tree that exercises the
# path hard enough to trip it.
#
# ── Why these three frames, and why not more ──────────────────────────────
#
# Each names a frame TSan resolves *by symbol* rather than by debug info,
# which is what makes them survive the runner's stripped `libstdc++.so.6`.
# All three are confirmed resolvable there: the first two appear by name in
# the CI report this file was written against
# (`std::__exception_ptr::exception_ptr::_M_release() <null>
# (libstdc++.so.6+0xc2f8c)` -- a resolved name with an unresolved file,
# exactly what an exported `.dynsym` symbol looks like after `strip`), and
# `__dest_thunk<T>` is a template instantiated into *our* binary, so it
# carries morph's own debug info.
#
# The third line is needed because TSan sometimes cannot unwind past
# libstdc++'s tail calls and prints a write stack that goes straight from
# `~runtime_error` to the morph frame, with no `_M_release` in between.
# Measured: with only the first two lines this test still reported 1 run in
# 700, and that one run was exactly this shape.
# `std::runtime_error::~runtime_error` is likewise exported and likewise
# appears resolved by name in CI's own report.
#
# Deliberately *not* suppressed, though an earlier draft had them:
# `__cxa_free_exception` (in no measured report against this tree) and
# `std::string::_Rep::_M_dispose` (an inlined libstdc++ internal that would
# not symbolize on the runner, so it would be a line that silently matched
# nothing). Also rejected: `race:morph::async::detail::CompletionState`,
# which is symbolization-proof but would hide any future genuine race
# involving that type.
#
# These are narrow on purpose. A genuine race elsewhere in morph does not
# destroy a `std::runtime_error` or touch `exception_ptr`'s refcount, and a
# planted control race was confirmed still reported with this file active.
race:std::__exception_ptr::exception_ptr::_M_release
race:std::__exception_ptr::__dest_thunk
race:std::runtime_error::~runtime_error
